WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.601 --> 00:00:03.034
- Hi, everyone. Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.034 --> 00:00:04.944
Welcome to the EDTalks,

00:00:04.944 --> 00:00:08.394
version of our Homeroom
with Sal live stream.

00:00:08.394 --> 00:00:10.514
We have a very exciting conversation today

00:00:10.514 --> 00:00:12.254
with LaVerne Srinivasan.

00:00:12.254 --> 00:00:13.924
But before we get into that conversation,

00:00:13.924 --> 00:00:15.564
I will give my standard reminders.

00:00:15.564 --> 00:00:17.434
First of all, we are not in for profit.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:19.214
We can only exist through donations

00:00:19.214 --> 00:00:20.264
from folks like yourself.

00:00:20.264 --> 00:00:21.954
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:25.394
please think about going
to Khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:25.394 --> 00:00:28.634
Donations of all sizes
make a huge difference

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:30.784
and allow us to deliver Khan Academy

00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:33.574
to hundreds of millions of
people around the world.

00:00:33.574 --> 00:00:35.064
I also wanna make a special shout out

00:00:35.064 --> 00:00:37.154
to several organizations that stepped up,

00:00:37.154 --> 00:00:39.494
especially during the
pandemic when they realized

00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:42.594
that more people than ever
we're leaning on Khan Academy,

00:00:42.594 --> 00:00:44.704
which also meant our costs were going up.

00:00:44.704 --> 00:00:47.254
Our server costs and
our desire to accelerate

00:00:47.254 --> 00:00:48.334
a whole bunch of programs.

00:00:48.334 --> 00:00:51.744
So special thanks to Bank of
America, AT&amp;T, Google.org,

00:00:51.744 --> 00:00:55.364
Novartis, Fastly and general motors.

00:00:55.364 --> 00:00:58.654
And then last but not least,
there is a podcast version

00:00:58.654 --> 00:01:01.884
of this live stream available
wherever you get your podcasts

00:01:01.884 --> 00:01:04.554
on "Homeroom with Sal the podcast".

00:01:04.554 --> 00:01:07.154
So definitely check that out.

00:01:07.154 --> 00:01:11.644
So with that, I am excited to
introduce LaVerne Srinivasan,

00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:14.254
who is Vice President
and Director of Education

00:01:14.254 --> 00:01:16.554
at the Carnegie Corporation.

00:01:16.554 --> 00:01:20.214
Full disclosure, also a
funder of Khan Academy.

00:01:20.214 --> 00:01:25.214
So I am somewhat biased, but
LaVerne, great to see you.

00:01:25.444 --> 00:01:27.224
Thanks for joining us.

00:01:27.224 --> 00:01:29.767
- Great to see you too,
Sal. Pleasure to be here.

00:01:31.264 --> 00:01:33.224
- Well, maybe a good place to start

00:01:33.224 --> 00:01:36.724
for those who haven't heard,
and I'm sure there aren't many,

00:01:36.724 --> 00:01:39.604
what does the Carnegie
Corporation of New York do?

00:01:39.604 --> 00:01:42.294
And what does the director of education do

00:01:42.294 --> 00:01:44.274
within that context?

00:01:44.274 --> 00:01:47.524
- Absolutely. Carnegie
Corporation of New York

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:50.734
is actually a foundation that has invested

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:55.734
in knowledge to inspire
informed actions since 1911.

00:01:55.864 --> 00:01:59.334
We actually invest in
democracy, international peace

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:01.794
and security and in education.

00:02:01.794 --> 00:02:05.914
And for my role in the education
program, we are focused

00:02:05.914 --> 00:02:10.564
on ensuring that our American
public education system serves

00:02:10.564 --> 00:02:12.974
all students by building
the knowledge, skills

00:02:12.974 --> 00:02:16.604
and dispositions that they
need to be active participants

00:02:16.604 --> 00:02:20.374
in our democracy and be able to thrive

00:02:20.374 --> 00:02:23.284
and engage fully in our economy.

00:02:23.284 --> 00:02:26.804
So we have five grant making portfolios,

00:02:26.804 --> 00:02:30.034
but our strategy is really
focused on making sure

00:02:30.034 --> 00:02:34.724
that parents and educators
can be full partners

00:02:34.724 --> 00:02:37.164
in transforming the learning experience.

00:02:37.164 --> 00:02:38.844
You think it's absolutely critical

00:02:38.844 --> 00:02:42.713
that our best hope for having equitable

00:02:42.713 --> 00:02:47.713
and transformed learning
environment is by fully engaging

00:02:48.114 --> 00:02:50.484
the adults and building their capacity

00:02:50.484 --> 00:02:53.644
and also ensuring that
they can work in a system

00:02:53.644 --> 00:02:56.504
that's optimized to ensure
their effectiveness,

00:02:56.504 --> 00:03:00.592
including making sure that
their actions are informed

00:03:00.592 --> 00:03:04.014
by knowledge that's been
developed in the field.

00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:08.644
So our five portfolios are,
new design students learning,

00:03:08.644 --> 00:03:12.124
where we focus on innovations
in classrooms and schools

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:16.604
to promote a better student
learning ,and youth development.

00:03:16.604 --> 00:03:20.084
We also focus on pathways
to post-secondary success

00:03:20.084 --> 00:03:23.904
where we re-imagined pathways
for high school graduates

00:03:23.904 --> 00:03:26.564
including access and completion of college

00:03:26.564 --> 00:03:30.904
and better aligning
K-12 higher and careers.

00:03:30.904 --> 00:03:34.284
We have another portfolio focused
on leadership and teaching

00:03:34.284 --> 00:03:36.144
to advanced learning, where we focus

00:03:36.144 --> 00:03:39.914
on high quality curriculum
and strengthening teaching

00:03:39.914 --> 00:03:43.184
and learning to implement

00:03:43.184 --> 00:03:46.944
that high quality standards
aligned, that curriculum,

00:03:46.944 --> 00:03:48.874
along with professional learning.

00:03:48.874 --> 00:03:53.194
We also have a portfolio that
focuses on family engagement.

00:03:53.194 --> 00:03:55.464
It's a public understanding portfolio,

00:03:55.464 --> 00:03:57.804
where we're really looking to
have a shared understanding

00:03:57.804 --> 00:04:01.494
of how all of the adults in
the system can work together

00:04:01.494 --> 00:04:05.084
along with students to transform
the learning environment.

00:04:05.084 --> 00:04:07.074
And lastly, we have an
integration learning

00:04:07.074 --> 00:04:08.694
and innovation portfolio.

00:04:08.694 --> 00:04:13.694
And in that portfolios where
we really inspire all folks

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:17.684
who are essential in transforming

00:04:17.684 --> 00:04:21.824
our nation's education
system to develop strategies

00:04:21.824 --> 00:04:24.723
and implement them in complex systems.

00:04:24.723 --> 00:04:27.664
And share the learning associated

00:04:27.664 --> 00:04:30.084
with that and foster collaboration.

00:04:30.084 --> 00:04:32.084
So those are our five portfolios though.

00:04:33.524 --> 00:04:36.094
- And that was super helpful.

00:04:36.094 --> 00:04:37.764
And I just wanna remind everyone watching,

00:04:37.764 --> 00:04:39.644
especially those who are watching live,

00:04:39.644 --> 00:04:41.564
if you have any questions
for Laverne or myself,

00:04:41.564 --> 00:04:42.454
we wanna get to them.

00:04:42.454 --> 00:04:44.144
So please put them in the message board

00:04:44.144 --> 00:04:45.354
under wherever you're watching this,

00:04:45.354 --> 00:04:47.384
Facebook, YouTube, wherever.

00:04:47.384 --> 00:04:51.134
But LaVerne, on this focus on education,

00:04:51.134 --> 00:04:53.974
I'm curious about where
you are most excited,

00:04:53.974 --> 00:04:56.024
where you're most hopeful and especially,

00:04:56.994 --> 00:05:00.424
has the last year changed
your perspective on anything

00:05:00.424 --> 00:05:03.654
given all of the disruption
that has happened

00:05:03.654 --> 00:05:05.617
to education because of the pandemic?

00:05:07.294 --> 00:05:11.894
- Yeah, so I feel we've really
reflected on this deeply

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:14.594
because there has been so much disruption

00:05:14.594 --> 00:05:19.594
and there are so many people
who are really struggling

00:05:19.824 --> 00:05:21.854
and have struggled, and also people

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:23.964
who have had incredible experiences.

00:05:23.964 --> 00:05:28.964
But within our work we realize
that so many of the aspects

00:05:29.484 --> 00:05:34.354
of the focus in our five
portfolios are turning out

00:05:34.354 --> 00:05:38.634
to be really important
aspects of places, schools,

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:43.284
school systems that have
been able to pivot and adjust

00:05:43.284 --> 00:05:47.304
and adapt effectively in
this disruptive environment.

00:05:47.304 --> 00:05:52.304
So in particular, I'd say
one of our newer portfolios

00:05:53.554 --> 00:05:56.084
although we've been
invested now for six years

00:05:56.084 --> 00:06:00.824
in family engagement,
that's been a real area

00:06:00.824 --> 00:06:05.584
of priority for us, and
one that has been essential

00:06:05.584 --> 00:06:09.634
in helping through our grantees
respond to the challenges

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:12.514
that families have faced with the tsunami

00:06:12.514 --> 00:06:15.024
of resources coming at them to the extent

00:06:15.024 --> 00:06:18.314
that they have them, but
also really connecting

00:06:18.314 --> 00:06:22.436
with their teachers, their
schools and school environments

00:06:22.436 --> 00:06:24.634
and our grantees have really,

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:29.634
I think stepped up incredibly
well to help all parents

00:06:29.674 --> 00:06:34.094
and schools, teachers
and educators connect

00:06:34.094 --> 00:06:36.144
with families in more meaningful ways.

00:06:36.144 --> 00:06:39.244
So we're excited about the possibilities

00:06:39.244 --> 00:06:44.244
that this has in continuing to
build commitment and to build

00:06:44.914 --> 00:06:48.284
on the strength of what's
happened over the last year,

00:06:48.284 --> 00:06:51.884
so that we'd no longer operate in a way

00:06:51.884 --> 00:06:55.124
that has some artificial
constraints and walls

00:06:55.124 --> 00:06:57.644
around schools that don't effectively,

00:06:57.644 --> 00:07:00.994
in the past did not effectively
engage parents as well,

00:07:00.994 --> 00:07:03.881
and communities as well
as they could have.

00:07:03.881 --> 00:07:06.174
- And we have two
questions that double click

00:07:06.174 --> 00:07:08.019
on what you just talked about.

00:07:08.019 --> 00:07:11.104
Both questions from YouTube,
Susanna Garcia Dominguez,

00:07:11.104 --> 00:07:12.974
who's a regular on the show, so to speak.

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:15.244
I almost view her as a co-host of sorts.

00:07:15.244 --> 00:07:17.404
But I've never met her in-person,

00:07:17.404 --> 00:07:19.364
but I look forward to one day.

00:07:19.364 --> 00:07:21.294
She says, hi LaVerne, please share some

00:07:21.294 --> 00:07:23.134
of the most innovative
projects you support.

00:07:23.134 --> 00:07:24.664
Thanks for all you do from YouTube

00:07:24.664 --> 00:07:28.454
Suehib Alashkar also saying, what are some

00:07:28.454 --> 00:07:29.914
of the projects you
support and in what way?

00:07:29.914 --> 00:07:32.644
So especially with this parent engagement,

00:07:32.644 --> 00:07:35.404
why do you think there
was a gap to begin with?

00:07:35.404 --> 00:07:37.774
And what are some of the efforts
that you all are supporting

00:07:37.774 --> 00:07:38.964
that you think it could close this gap?

00:07:38.964 --> 00:07:40.434
Because it does seem in some ways obvious

00:07:40.434 --> 00:07:43.554
that like the parent is an
incredibly important actor

00:07:43.554 --> 00:07:44.754
for children's learning.

00:07:45.764 --> 00:07:49.624
- Yeah, I think we historically
focused in school systems

00:07:49.624 --> 00:07:53.454
on parent engagement is just an activity

00:07:53.454 --> 00:07:58.054
where we were inviting parents
to come at the very least

00:07:58.054 --> 00:08:01.664
for parent-teacher conferences,
maybe for bake sales

00:08:01.664 --> 00:08:02.934
and other activities.

00:08:02.934 --> 00:08:05.174
But there hasn't always been, and this is,

00:08:05.174 --> 00:08:06.864
you can't paint the entire country

00:08:06.864 --> 00:08:08.764
with one broad brush stroke around this.

00:08:08.764 --> 00:08:11.424
But you know, I think
that a lot of efforts

00:08:11.424 --> 00:08:15.414
were involving bringing parents
into the school environment

00:08:15.414 --> 00:08:19.964
to participate in some
particularly focused way.

00:08:19.964 --> 00:08:22.784
And I think the difference
in what we have seen

00:08:22.784 --> 00:08:26.194
in the last year, and by
the way, research has not,

00:08:26.194 --> 00:08:28.324
we know through research
for quite some time

00:08:28.324 --> 00:08:30.924
that parent engagement is essential

00:08:30.924 --> 00:08:32.554
to improving student outcomes.

00:08:32.554 --> 00:08:35.584
So what we've seen is
for example, you know,

00:08:35.584 --> 00:08:39.364
efforts that we supported,
that homeschool connection.

00:08:39.364 --> 00:08:42.204
Engagement isn't just about you know,

00:08:42.204 --> 00:08:44.404
showing up in schools on school time.

00:08:44.404 --> 00:08:47.974
Parents have busy schedules,
not every parent can come

00:08:47.974 --> 00:08:51.111
in at the prescribed times
that schools invite them in.

00:08:51.111 --> 00:08:53.914
So how do we create an environment

00:08:53.914 --> 00:08:56.404
where there is a more dynamic engagement

00:08:56.404 --> 00:08:57.904
between home and school?

00:08:57.904 --> 00:09:00.404
One of our grantees that is really strong

00:09:00.404 --> 00:09:03.234
in this area is PowerMyLearning,
where they've created

00:09:03.234 --> 00:09:07.244
an innovative app, that
allows children to demonstrate

00:09:07.244 --> 00:09:10.084
what they're learning
in school by actually,

00:09:10.084 --> 00:09:13.734
basically teaching it
to a caregiver or parent

00:09:13.734 --> 00:09:16.914
or other adult in their homes,

00:09:16.914 --> 00:09:18.354
outside of the school environment.

00:09:18.354 --> 00:09:23.354
So it really triangulates
in a way that home, school,

00:09:23.804 --> 00:09:28.804
teacher, parent and student
learning and creates a more,

00:09:30.984 --> 00:09:35.475
you know, bi-directional
engagement that fosters

00:09:35.475 --> 00:09:37.644
a more inclusive learning environment.

00:09:37.644 --> 00:09:39.694
So those are the kinds
of things we can do.

00:09:39.694 --> 00:09:43.974
I mean, during the pandemic,
there were also, you know,

00:09:43.974 --> 00:09:46.864
efforts that helped with
translation, you know,

00:09:46.864 --> 00:09:51.094
or how parents understand how
to read to their children,

00:09:51.094 --> 00:09:53.734
like springboard collaborative.

00:09:53.734 --> 00:09:56.454
So there are, it's a matter of fact

00:09:56.454 --> 00:09:59.634
we actually have a number of
these resources on our website

00:09:59.634 --> 00:10:03.814
that we tried to help
curate for busy families

00:10:03.814 --> 00:10:06.724
who also couldn't work through
the tsunami of resources

00:10:06.724 --> 00:10:08.524
and started flowing their way.

00:10:08.524 --> 00:10:11.454
So I think the real secret,
Sal, and what I hope

00:10:11.454 --> 00:10:14.714
we will see looking
different going forward,

00:10:14.714 --> 00:10:19.034
is a better understanding
of where families are,

00:10:19.034 --> 00:10:22.664
and meeting them where they
are in a more authentic way

00:10:22.664 --> 00:10:26.907
as opposed to just let us
invite you into our four walls.

00:10:27.914 --> 00:10:29.274
- Now I love that example you gave.

00:10:29.274 --> 00:10:31.754
You know, I've always been a
huge advocate and supporter

00:10:31.754 --> 00:10:35.434
that some students could
actually be incredible teachers

00:10:36.284 --> 00:10:38.494
at least tutors, maybe I should say.

00:10:38.494 --> 00:10:40.374
And to maybe leverage
them within a classroom

00:10:40.374 --> 00:10:43.364
to tutor each other or
to tutor younger peers.

00:10:43.364 --> 00:10:45.474
But it sounds like y'all
are taking it further

00:10:45.474 --> 00:10:47.774
with some of your grantees to say,

00:10:47.774 --> 00:10:50.294
well, why can't the students
tutor their parents?

00:10:50.294 --> 00:10:52.774
Which I think is a very,
very, very powerful idea,

00:10:52.774 --> 00:10:53.764
because it is.

00:10:53.764 --> 00:10:55.604
You know, I always cringe when a parent,

00:10:55.604 --> 00:10:57.284
but I understand at the
same time when they say,

00:10:57.284 --> 00:10:59.324
Oh, well, I haven't seen
this since fourth grade.

00:10:59.324 --> 00:11:01.454
I was like, yeah, you went to
fifth grade and sixth grade.

00:11:01.454 --> 00:11:04.834
But there is sometimes this
fear of parents to even engage,

00:11:04.834 --> 00:11:07.474
but this almost, this
kind of flips the things

00:11:07.474 --> 00:11:10.454
in a really interesting way.

00:11:10.454 --> 00:11:11.287
What are your...

00:11:11.287 --> 00:11:13.104
You know, you've been in
the field of philanthropy,

00:11:13.104 --> 00:11:15.224
in the field of education for a while.

00:11:15.224 --> 00:11:20.224
And you know, I like
you, I'm very optimistic

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.514
about where the world can go,

00:11:22.514 --> 00:11:25.344
but there's also sometimes
cynicism that people

00:11:25.344 --> 00:11:27.174
have been working on
this problem for a while.

00:11:27.174 --> 00:11:29.204
They've been throwing
money at different parts

00:11:29.204 --> 00:11:32.524
of the problem, but at least
from some vantage points

00:11:32.524 --> 00:11:35.334
it looks like it hasn't moved the dial.

00:11:35.334 --> 00:11:37.544
Do you agree with some of that cynicism?

00:11:37.544 --> 00:11:39.057
Do you have some fears?

00:11:39.917 --> 00:11:43.414
And how the philanthropic world do y'all,

00:11:43.414 --> 00:11:45.624
maybe even learn from some of, you know,

00:11:45.624 --> 00:11:46.744
it might not be things y'all have done,

00:11:46.744 --> 00:11:48.574
but it's stuff that had
happened in the past

00:11:48.574 --> 00:11:50.024
and what are those learnings?

00:11:51.334 --> 00:11:53.387
- Yeah, that's a great question, Sal.

00:11:54.224 --> 00:11:56.624
You're right, I want to be optimistic,

00:11:56.624 --> 00:11:58.344
but I also wanna be realistic.

00:11:58.344 --> 00:12:03.294
And we really have a big
challenge in front of us.

00:12:03.294 --> 00:12:04.714
I found myself thinking about,

00:12:04.714 --> 00:12:06.924
we have to get out of our comfort zones.

00:12:06.924 --> 00:12:09.834
We all have to grapple with new ideas

00:12:09.834 --> 00:12:13.294
and engage ineffectively
a design challenge

00:12:13.294 --> 00:12:17.374
where instead of gravitating
to just siloed solutions

00:12:17.374 --> 00:12:20.054
that we really start to
look at the full ecosystem

00:12:20.054 --> 00:12:23.954
of learning, and the in-center
children and families

00:12:23.954 --> 00:12:28.794
in that as authentic partners
in it and designers in it.

00:12:28.794 --> 00:12:31.304
But also to recognize that, you know,

00:12:31.304 --> 00:12:36.304
we can't all in the education
sector, you know, get attached

00:12:36.324 --> 00:12:39.354
to the thing that we think
is the most effective

00:12:39.354 --> 00:12:43.274
or likely to be the most
highly effective solution

00:12:43.274 --> 00:12:46.544
to addressing the change we
need to see in education,

00:12:46.544 --> 00:12:48.664
or to better meet student learning needs.

00:12:48.664 --> 00:12:51.034
So I think one of the
things we have to learn

00:12:51.034 --> 00:12:55.744
is less fragmentation, more collaboration,

00:12:55.744 --> 00:12:59.714
much better sharing of
learning, understanding

00:12:59.714 --> 00:13:02.164
that it's not just what we do that matters

00:13:02.164 --> 00:13:04.384
but how we do it that matters.

00:13:04.384 --> 00:13:07.304
Those I think are going to
be essential in making change

00:13:07.304 --> 00:13:10.014
and you know, aligning resources,

00:13:10.014 --> 00:13:14.141
aligning that highly effective curriculum

00:13:15.464 --> 00:13:17.094
with aligned professional learning.

00:13:17.094 --> 00:13:21.294
Keeping and considering that
adults and students make up

00:13:21.294 --> 00:13:24.287
that learning environment
that we really need

00:13:24.287 --> 00:13:29.162
to transform to really lean into a future

00:13:29.162 --> 00:13:32.854
that is gonna thrive on lifelong learning.

00:13:32.854 --> 00:13:35.064
And so if our learning environments

00:13:35.064 --> 00:13:38.804
aren't more porous inside of
school, outside of school,

00:13:38.804 --> 00:13:42.394
at home, learning happens
in all of those environments

00:13:42.394 --> 00:13:47.394
and how we really take ourselves
out of our comfort zone

00:13:47.424 --> 00:13:52.424
to really think and imagine a
way that we can knit together

00:13:53.264 --> 00:13:56.894
the positive and promising
proof points and solutions

00:13:56.894 --> 00:14:01.894
that we've seen to actually
support adults and students

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:07.040
to thrive in a transformed
and more equitable

00:14:07.444 --> 00:14:11.097
learning environment and
a learning ecosystem.

00:14:12.424 --> 00:14:15.294
- Yeah, it sounds like one of
the key takeaways has been,

00:14:15.294 --> 00:14:17.434
it's not just what happens...

00:14:17.434 --> 00:14:18.544
Obviously what happens in the classroom

00:14:18.544 --> 00:14:20.464
is incredibly important,
but what goes on beyond

00:14:20.464 --> 00:14:22.014
the classroom, especially the family.

00:14:22.014 --> 00:14:24.954
And you know, related to
that, the pandemic introduced

00:14:24.954 --> 00:14:27.904
a lot of disruption, but
people explored things

00:14:27.904 --> 00:14:30.894
like pod schooling and
homeschooling and distance learning.

00:14:30.894 --> 00:14:33.524
And for a lot of folks that
didn't work, but for some

00:14:33.524 --> 00:14:35.204
it seems like at least parts of it might.

00:14:35.204 --> 00:14:36.884
And related to that, there's
a question from YouTube.

00:14:36.884 --> 00:14:39.944
Strava Padwell asks, "Hey,
LaVerne what do you think

00:14:39.944 --> 00:14:42.084
about homeschool learning
and normal school?"

00:14:42.084 --> 00:14:42.944
And I'll just add to that.

00:14:42.944 --> 00:14:46.084
Do you think we're we're kind
of gonna start hybridizing

00:14:46.084 --> 00:14:47.884
in certain ways, you know,

00:14:47.884 --> 00:14:49.624
that's kind of the
ultimate bringing the home

00:14:49.624 --> 00:14:50.574
into the classroom?

00:14:51.694 --> 00:14:53.114
- Yeah, I think that,

00:14:54.294 --> 00:14:57.874
I think that what happened
with pods is really,

00:14:57.874 --> 00:14:59.184
really compelling.

00:14:59.184 --> 00:15:01.784
In many ways, you know, I
think people are focused

00:15:01.784 --> 00:15:03.444
on the fact that there were pods,

00:15:03.444 --> 00:15:05.744
but what did pods represent?

00:15:05.744 --> 00:15:10.094
I mean, pods represented
another opportunity

00:15:10.094 --> 00:15:15.094
to bring community and families together.

00:15:15.214 --> 00:15:18.524
Again, understanding
better what's necessary

00:15:18.524 --> 00:15:21.104
to support their students
and their children

00:15:21.104 --> 00:15:25.004
and their learning and a
little bit more of a strategy

00:15:25.004 --> 00:15:27.744
around how you can focus
that in smaller groups.

00:15:27.744 --> 00:15:30.874
Well, we can learn from
that both in the classroom,

00:15:30.874 --> 00:15:34.944
but we can also learn that, not
everyone learns the same way

00:15:37.882 --> 00:15:39.517
Paying attention to the individual
needs of students allows

00:15:42.544 --> 00:15:45.604
you all of a sudden to
open your mind and realize

00:15:45.604 --> 00:15:50.404
that learning in classrooms
can be approached differently.

00:15:50.404 --> 00:15:54.534
And we do it now, that it
happens in small pods in schools

00:15:54.534 --> 00:15:58.054
with attention from
one teacher and ability

00:15:58.054 --> 00:16:02.071
to accelerate learning while
others are finishing learning

00:16:02.071 --> 00:16:06.004
hat they're not as fully advanced in yet.

00:16:06.004 --> 00:16:09.184
So I think that, you know,
I think homeschooling

00:16:09.184 --> 00:16:11.884
is something that works
extraordinarily well

00:16:11.884 --> 00:16:13.214
for some families.

00:16:13.214 --> 00:16:16.394
And I think families know
best what's gonna work

00:16:16.394 --> 00:16:17.564
for their kids.

00:16:17.564 --> 00:16:20.784
But I think again, that
making it more porous,

00:16:20.784 --> 00:16:24.014
realizing that there's
something that we can all learn

00:16:24.014 --> 00:16:27.514
by centering the needs
of students and the needs

00:16:27.514 --> 00:16:30.764
that specific students
have for their learning

00:16:30.764 --> 00:16:33.904
and to advance not only
their academic development

00:16:33.904 --> 00:16:36.774
but their socio-emotional
development as well,

00:16:36.774 --> 00:16:39.294
I think are what's gonna
make the difference

00:16:39.294 --> 00:16:43.134
in whether we learn from
this experience and advance

00:16:43.134 --> 00:16:47.214
our conception of what learning
is and where it takes place,

00:16:47.214 --> 00:16:51.414
or whether we stay rooted
in a compliance oriented

00:16:51.414 --> 00:16:54.067
industrial age education system.

00:16:55.134 --> 00:16:55.967
- Makes a lot of sense.

00:16:55.967 --> 00:16:57.014
And there's another question here.

00:16:57.014 --> 00:16:58.744
I guess it's a question for both of us.

00:16:58.744 --> 00:17:01.211
From Facebook Mila Nalani Prabhu says,

00:17:01.211 --> 00:17:04.604
"Hi, Sal and LaVerne,
please list down in order

00:17:04.604 --> 00:17:07.734
what I as a parent tutor
can do for student success."

00:17:07.734 --> 00:17:10.794
So I'll let you take a stab
at it. I have some ideas too

00:17:12.344 --> 00:17:14.484
- As a student tutor.

00:17:14.484 --> 00:17:16.304
Well, I guess it depends on
what they're tutoring in.

00:17:16.304 --> 00:17:17.741
I think that it's important.

00:17:17.741 --> 00:17:19.087
The parent or the tutor?

00:17:20.024 --> 00:17:21.194
She said she is a parent.

00:17:21.194 --> 00:17:23.414
What can I do like right
now to help my kids?

00:17:23.414 --> 00:17:24.734
- Exactly. Okay.

00:17:24.734 --> 00:17:29.174
I think there's some really
great resources out there.

00:17:29.174 --> 00:17:31.494
I think Sal is probably gonna give us some

00:17:31.494 --> 00:17:33.434
of those also from his perspective.

00:17:33.434 --> 00:17:36.804
That wide open school, which
is actually one of our grantees

00:17:36.804 --> 00:17:39.374
has some incredible resources
that were curated there

00:17:39.374 --> 00:17:41.044
for parents and activities.

00:17:41.044 --> 00:17:44.354
And depending on the
level your child is at,

00:17:44.354 --> 00:17:47.214
you will find what, you
know, different resources

00:17:47.214 --> 00:17:50.265
that will help you understand
what should my child know

00:17:50.265 --> 00:17:53.906
at this level and what
activities or other strategies

00:17:53.906 --> 00:17:58.834
can I use to make sure
that I help them at home,

00:17:58.834 --> 00:18:02.404
continue to advance and
learn along the pathway

00:18:02.404 --> 00:18:03.434
that they're on.

00:18:03.434 --> 00:18:06.284
In addition, there are
also other resources

00:18:06.284 --> 00:18:09.164
whether it's a great schools.

00:18:09.164 --> 00:18:12.634
Again, I invite you to go
to our website as well,

00:18:12.634 --> 00:18:14.704
not as a commercial for our website,

00:18:14.704 --> 00:18:17.524
but to say we've tried to
thoughtfully curate some

00:18:17.524 --> 00:18:21.031
of these things that we think
will help parents navigate.

00:18:21.031 --> 00:18:23.114
And if you're having young children,

00:18:23.114 --> 00:18:24.795
reading is so essential.

00:18:24.795 --> 00:18:29.384
Having the opportunity to
read with your child is one

00:18:29.384 --> 00:18:32.927
of the most critical things
that you can certainly do.

00:18:34.324 --> 00:18:35.157
Sal.

00:18:35.157 --> 00:18:39.317
- Yeah, I love all of
those and I will add things

00:18:39.317 --> 00:18:41.344
that will not surprise anyone.

00:18:41.344 --> 00:18:44.784
But you know, Khan Academy
as I tell everybody

00:18:44.784 --> 00:18:46.824
who will listen, if you're able
to work with their children

00:18:46.824 --> 00:18:49.754
I don't know how old they are,
but we go down all the way

00:18:49.754 --> 00:18:51.834
to Khan Academy kids all the way through,

00:18:51.834 --> 00:18:54.624
especially Khan Academy kids
covers math, reading, writing,

00:18:54.624 --> 00:18:56.794
social emotional learning,
but then, you know,

00:18:56.794 --> 00:18:58.634
math we are very comprehensive all the way

00:18:58.634 --> 00:19:01.504
through middle high school,
college level mathematics.

00:19:01.504 --> 00:19:03.564
And they're pretty consistent.

00:19:03.564 --> 00:19:04.954
And you know, we have 50 efficacy studies

00:19:04.954 --> 00:19:07.444
that have students even
put in 45 minutes a week.

00:19:07.444 --> 00:19:11.544
It grows them 20, 30 in some
cases, 50% more than expected.

00:19:11.544 --> 00:19:13.944
So if your child is able to put in,

00:19:13.944 --> 00:19:17.044
I would say 15 minutes a
day, which is very doable,

00:19:17.044 --> 00:19:19.924
you know, and you can help
motivate them as a parent.

00:19:19.924 --> 00:19:22.094
In two or three months I will be surprised

00:19:22.094 --> 00:19:24.294
if you don't see a massive difference.

00:19:24.294 --> 00:19:27.084
Otherwise I will give a
personal money back guarantee.

00:19:27.084 --> 00:19:27.917
Partially joking.

00:19:27.917 --> 00:19:29.304
But even if we did charge you,

00:19:29.304 --> 00:19:32.204
I would stand behind that
with a money back guarantee.

00:19:32.204 --> 00:19:34.501
And the other is effort-

00:19:35.394 --> 00:19:37.177
- I can vouch as a parent.
- Go ahead.

00:19:37.177 --> 00:19:39.304
- As a parent I've
actually used Khan Academy

00:19:39.304 --> 00:19:42.555
with my child as well.

00:19:42.555 --> 00:19:44.924
- Oh, no, I actually, we've
talked about that before.

00:19:44.924 --> 00:19:47.064
And I actually that I
think is our most powerful.

00:19:47.064 --> 00:19:48.864
You know, even when we
talked to philanthropists,

00:19:48.864 --> 00:19:51.024
if the philanthropy sees
that works for their family,

00:19:51.024 --> 00:19:54.026
they're like, Hey it could
work for a lot of folks family.

00:19:54.026 --> 00:19:57.026
But the other effort is
as an effort which is,

00:19:57.026 --> 00:19:59.014
that you could do this as
a spin off of Khan Academy

00:19:59.014 --> 00:20:01.254
or complimentary effort
called schoolhouse.world

00:20:01.254 --> 00:20:02.087
for free tutoring.

00:20:02.087 --> 00:20:04.284
So if your child is 13 or over,

00:20:04.284 --> 00:20:06.534
they can either get free tutoring .

00:20:06.534 --> 00:20:08.241
Right now it's math and SAT,
but it's going to expand.

00:20:08.241 --> 00:20:10.604
And actually it's
expanded into the sciences

00:20:10.604 --> 00:20:12.094
and AP subjects as well.

00:20:12.094 --> 00:20:15.154
But also if once they
really master concepts,

00:20:15.154 --> 00:20:18.604
they can give tutoring to
other people is analogous

00:20:18.604 --> 00:20:20.324
to what LaVerne was
saying about the power.

00:20:20.324 --> 00:20:22.784
When you teach others not
only are you helping them,

00:20:22.784 --> 00:20:24.544
but you're helping yourself.

00:20:24.544 --> 00:20:26.904
So there's a lot of resources now

00:20:26.904 --> 00:20:28.792
and it's just about making sense of them

00:20:28.792 --> 00:20:30.674
and doing them consistently.

00:20:30.674 --> 00:20:32.844
You know, it's all about
we're all guilty of like,

00:20:32.844 --> 00:20:35.004
I'm gonna play a guitar and
I'm gonna do 10 hours today,

00:20:35.004 --> 00:20:36.604
and then it drifts away.

00:20:36.604 --> 00:20:39.154
But it's all about if you
do 15, 20 minutes a day

00:20:39.154 --> 00:20:41.834
for a long period of time
it makes a huge difference.

00:20:41.834 --> 00:20:44.494
So LaVerne and these
conversations always go faster

00:20:44.494 --> 00:20:47.014
than I expect, but I also wanna you know,

00:20:47.014 --> 00:20:48.894
kind of understand a little
bit of your background.

00:20:48.894 --> 00:20:51.464
What got you into this field?

00:20:51.464 --> 00:20:55.177
How did you get into philanthropy,
education in particular?

00:20:57.423 --> 00:20:59.294
How did you get here?

00:20:59.294 --> 00:21:02.529
- Sure, well, I was
fortunate enough to grow up

00:21:02.529 --> 00:21:07.164
in a family that absolutely
thought education

00:21:07.164 --> 00:21:08.614
was the most important thing.

00:21:08.614 --> 00:21:11.764
And I'm so grateful every day for that.

00:21:11.764 --> 00:21:15.584
And that has had me on a lifelong journey,

00:21:15.584 --> 00:21:20.064
whether it was tutoring
younger kids or working

00:21:20.064 --> 00:21:23.074
while I was in college
to set up opportunities

00:21:23.074 --> 00:21:26.484
in communities to work with
young people, with computers

00:21:26.484 --> 00:21:29.864
and helping them do exactly
what we're talking about now,

00:21:29.864 --> 00:21:32.794
which is sort of out of school
learning, that's something,

00:21:32.794 --> 00:21:33.844
or being a mentor.

00:21:33.844 --> 00:21:36.324
That those are all things that I did,

00:21:36.324 --> 00:21:39.004
while I wasn't in education as a career.

00:21:39.004 --> 00:21:41.924
But what inspired me
to get into education,

00:21:41.924 --> 00:21:46.034
stay in education is
actually using my skills

00:21:46.034 --> 00:21:50.304
and background from private
sector experience that I had,

00:21:50.304 --> 00:21:54.204
and to think about how
to transform the system.

00:21:54.204 --> 00:21:57.314
And transforming a complex
system like education

00:21:57.314 --> 00:21:59.234
is really hard to do,

00:21:59.234 --> 00:22:02.884
but it gets a little bit more realistic

00:22:02.884 --> 00:22:06.304
to believe it can be done,
which is what I've seen

00:22:06.304 --> 00:22:10.094
when you actually center
students and families

00:22:10.094 --> 00:22:13.607
and communities, and you
design from that perspective.

00:22:14.602 --> 00:22:18.994
You really have to stretch
ourselves to get out

00:22:18.994 --> 00:22:22.444
of our compliance-oriented
approach to education,

00:22:22.444 --> 00:22:26.044
because that's not the
expansive learning environment

00:22:26.044 --> 00:22:28.374
and ecosystem we need to succeed.

00:22:28.374 --> 00:22:30.744
So that my family inspired me.

00:22:30.744 --> 00:22:33.354
My teachers inspired me.

00:22:33.354 --> 00:22:37.294
Those who brought everything
to life in the classroom,

00:22:37.294 --> 00:22:40.454
whether it was in an English
class or a science class

00:22:41.304 --> 00:22:43.154
all of that has led me to say

00:22:43.154 --> 00:22:45.884
I want everyone to have
the opportunity that I feel

00:22:45.884 --> 00:22:47.684
like I was fortunate enough to have.

00:22:48.974 --> 00:22:50.734
- And on that last point as we ramp

00:22:50.734 --> 00:22:54.174
into teacher appreciation
week, I'm curious,

00:22:54.174 --> 00:22:56.774
are there any teachers that you...

00:22:56.774 --> 00:22:58.144
I'm sure many teachers
that you wanna appreciate,

00:22:58.144 --> 00:22:59.844
but any that come to mind that, you know,

00:22:59.844 --> 00:23:02.077
you really made a lasting impact on you?

00:23:03.464 --> 00:23:08.464
- Yeah, I will say, I wanna
appreciate all teachers

00:23:08.604 --> 00:23:11.904
just because one of the
things that I've done

00:23:11.904 --> 00:23:14.364
in education is really focused

00:23:14.364 --> 00:23:16.154
on how important teachers are.

00:23:16.154 --> 00:23:20.004
So first shout out to all
teachers, you are all special.

00:23:20.004 --> 00:23:22.784
And I hope in this time
of COVID, when everyone

00:23:22.784 --> 00:23:25.834
has had to get so much
closer to understanding

00:23:25.834 --> 00:23:28.974
what teaching is all about and
what it means for their kids,

00:23:28.974 --> 00:23:31.254
that we all appreciate all of you more.

00:23:31.254 --> 00:23:33.744
In particular I'll shout out Ronny Clemmer

00:23:33.744 --> 00:23:35.014
and Beverly Hannah.

00:23:35.014 --> 00:23:37.137
They were both English teachers of mine.

00:23:38.764 --> 00:23:40.424
One in middle school
and one in high school.

00:23:40.424 --> 00:23:43.314
And they just brought
every book to life for me,

00:23:43.314 --> 00:23:48.314
made it relevant, made it
engaging, and really contributed

00:23:48.894 --> 00:23:52.187
to sort of my lifelong love of reading.

00:23:54.324 --> 00:23:55.157
- Now that's great.

00:23:55.157 --> 00:23:57.574
I mean, I think the, you know,
the teachers in our lives

00:23:57.574 --> 00:23:59.114
that have made a difference.

00:23:59.114 --> 00:24:00.154
Yeah, I mean, it just keeps going

00:24:00.154 --> 00:24:03.024
and unfortunately they don't
hear probably about it enough,

00:24:03.024 --> 00:24:04.764
'cause, you know, maybe when
you were in middle school

00:24:04.764 --> 00:24:07.304
or high school, we're
not always there to say,

00:24:07.304 --> 00:24:08.724
Hey teacher you've changed my life,

00:24:08.724 --> 00:24:11.164
but then when we're in our 30s
and 40s and 50s, we're like,

00:24:11.164 --> 00:24:14.294
Wow, it'd be incredible to tell them that.

00:24:14.294 --> 00:24:16.734
Well, LaVerne, thank you so much

00:24:16.734 --> 00:24:18.124
for being part of this conversation.

00:24:18.124 --> 00:24:19.644
I hope it's the first of many.

00:24:19.644 --> 00:24:22.374
And I look forward to our
partnership, our journey,

00:24:22.374 --> 00:24:25.664
doing all of the things that
we talked about and more.

00:24:25.664 --> 00:24:26.994
And as we said is, you know,

00:24:26.994 --> 00:24:28.904
there's sometimes people
get cynical about education,

00:24:28.904 --> 00:24:30.504
but I've never been more optimistic.

00:24:30.504 --> 00:24:31.667
So thank you so much.

00:24:32.564 --> 00:24:35.034
- Well, it is such a
pleasure to be on the show

00:24:35.034 --> 00:24:36.214
and to work with you, Sal.

00:24:36.214 --> 00:24:37.564
So thank you for having me,

